operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComparing $A$ with the rotation matrix, we have \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta >0$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta <0,$  we must have that $\\theta $ lies in $2$nd quadrant. \n\nDividing the first equation by the second gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-1.\n$$\n\nThe principal value of ${\\theta }_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nbut this is outside the range $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\pi .$ To find a value in the required range, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\theta ={\\theta }_{1}+\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nFinally, to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/assets/49ec5421325f8ae5492743b988b1c0d727b2eac9e56f169ad5d55753bae5d69a.png)" 13194139535145 true] [17592186333720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333721 13194139535145 true] [17592186333720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333722 13194139535145 true] [17592186333720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333723 13194139535145 true] [17592186333720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333724 13194139535145 true] [17592186333720 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333725 13194139535145 true] [17592186333720 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535145 true] [17592186333720 1041 "selection" 13194139535145 true] [17592186333720 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333722 13194139535145 true] [17592186333720 1110 #uuid "0450729f-3962-529b-a848-0416eab70294" 13194139535145 true] [17592186333721 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-ca